.TH OPEN_FILE 3ggl "11 August 1997" "GGL/IGP ETHZ" "GGL Common Lib"
.SH NAME
open_text_file, open_binary_file \- enhanced file opening
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <common/inout.h>

.RB "FILE *" open_text_file
.RI "(char *" fname ", char *" mode ");"

.RB "FILE *" open_binary_file
.RI "(char *" fname ", char *" mode ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The function 
.B open_text_file()
and
.B open_binary_file()
open a file for operations in the
.I mode
specified. On Linux systems, those two functions behave identically.
They have mainly been introduced for porting reasons, so the
main programs using the library functions do not have to be altered.
In addition to the operations performed by
.BR fopen (3),
these functions will print an error message, when a file could not
be opened properly.

The parameter
.I mode
may be one of the following strings:
.RS
"r" for reading,
.RE
.RS
"w" for writing,
.RE
.RS
"w+" for appending,
.RE
.RS
"r+" for reading and writing,
.RE
.RS
.RB "or any other mode accepted by " fopen "(3)."
.RE

.SH "RETURN VALUES"
.BR open_text_file() " and " open_binary_file()
return a pointer to the stream of the file
.I fname
in the case it could be opened in the desired
.IR mode .
If an error occurred while
opening the file, these functions will return a
.B NULL
pointer. 
.SH ERRORS
The functions will call standard library functions
that may set
.IR errno .
If any called function produces an error, the functions
will print an appropriate error message to
.BR stderr .
.SH CAVEATS
You have to compile your program using
.I -lcommon
as a compiler flag to properly link
your function to the library.
